  "query_preview": "..\n\nSynoptic Setup: Forecast begins with a ridge running from northern\nManitoba into Ontario and down the Mississippi Valley. It then \nturns back west across the southern plains and into the Hot High \npressure over the SW. A broad trough over western half of Canada \nhas begun to break into two parts with the eastern trough running \ndown the Canadian plains and into the Dakotas. The western part begins\nover roughly British Columbia and moves off shore of the Pacific \nNorthwest and northern California. \n\nToday through Monday: A broad trough to the north and the Hot \nHigh pressure to the south will force flow across Montana to\nremain mostly zonal through these periods. This will bring some\nmid to high clouds but leave mostly cooler temps in the wake of \nthe cold front this morning that will hold on through Monday. By\nMonday morning a disturbance arrives in the flow that may have \nsome minor impacts of the SE side of the Yellowstone Valley with \na couple hundredths of an inch of rain. \n\nMonday night into Wednesday: The western most trough will move \ninland and dig into the Intermountain West and Four Corners. In \nresponse the northern section of the flow will build up into a \nridge over Montana that will keep conditions mostly dry through \nthese periods. Tuesday specifically will need to monitored for \nFire Weather concerns due to Low RH and gusty winds as the ridge \nbegins to break down.\n\nWednesday night onwards: Flow will attempt to return to zonal \nbriefly. But, model ensembles begin to break apart on individual\nwaves within the flow with more GFS runs focused on a brief \nridge/trough couplet, while EC members slowly orientate flow more\nto the northwest. Standard deviations amongst ensemble members \nsuggest that no particular run is favored. Therefore, confidence \nin these periods is low with the expectation of changes in future \niterations of the forecast here onward. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNo deviations to NBM were observed.",
